# fastly_ngwaf_signal_rule (Resource)

Manages a Fastly Next-Gen WAF `signal` rule defined at account scope: it
excludes a signal from requests that match its conditions, which is how a false
positive on a known-safe path is suppressed in every workspace named in
`applies_to`.

A signal rule has exactly one action, and that action only ever excludes a
signal, so `action` takes just the `signal` to exclude.

`applies_to` takes either a set of workspace IDs or the single entry `*`, which
applies the rule to every workspace in the account. It is a request body field
rather than a path segment, so adding or removing a workspace updates the rule
in place rather than replacing it.

Every rule must define at least one `condition`, `group_condition`, or
`multival_condition`, and no more than 10 of them combined - a
`group_condition` or `multival_condition` counts as one entry however many
conditions it nests.

Account scope also supports `request` rules, managed by
`fastly_ngwaf_request_rule`. Both are read back by the `fastly_ngwaf_rules` data
source.

## Example Usage

```terraform
resource "fastly_ngwaf_signal_rule" "exclude_xss_signal" {
  applies_to  = ["*"]
  description = "Exclude XSS signal to address a false positive"
  enabled     = true

  condition {
    field    = "path"
    operator = "like"
    value    = "/contact-form"
  }

  action {
    signal = "XSS"
  }
}
```

### Limited to named workspaces

```terraform
resource "fastly_ngwaf_workspace" "staging" {
  name        = "Example Staging Workspace"
  description = "Managed by Terraform"
  mode        = "log"

  attack_signal_thresholds {}
}

resource "fastly_ngwaf_signal_rule" "exclude_in_staging" {
  applies_to  = [fastly_ngwaf_workspace.staging.id]
  description = "Exclude a signal that only misfires in staging"
  enabled     = true

  condition {
    field    = "path"
    operator = "equals"
    value    = "/test-fixtures"
  }

  action {
    signal = "SQLI"
  }
}
```

